+## Bound the holder, not only the waiter: flock's payload inherits the lock fd (agents-etvw)
+
+`flock` gives the lock to the WHOLE process tree, not to the command that asked for it: the payload INHERITS the
+lock fd, so a `git send-pack` grandchild stuck on the network still holds the VM-wide lock after the tool that took
+it has gone. Observed 2026-10-14: a `--force-with-lease` stuck in send-pack, every other lane's `bd` call queued
+behind `flock -w 300`, and a bounded command dying at its ceiling with one rc 124 after 240s.
+**THE INHERITANCE IS WHAT MAKES THE LOCK AN EXCLUSION, so it is the thing to keep rather than tidy away.** The rule
+is BOUND THE HOLDER, NOT ONLY THE WAITER - and bound it by running the payload in its OWN PROCESS GROUP (setsid,
+then TERM to that group and, after a grace, KILL to that group), so the last fd closes BY CONSTRUCTION and the
+release is real rather than announced. The waiter is bounded with `timeout -k`; the WORK holds the fd for as long as
+the work is alive, so the lock is never released under a live `bd` and killing a HOLDER cannot let two runs share
+the Dolt DB.
+**`-o` IS THE TEMPTING WRONG ANSWER**, and the measurement is exactly why it tempts: driven both ways, WITHOUT `-o`
+the lock is STILL HELD after the payload exits, and WITH `-o` it is FREE. That passes the BOUND test while REMOVING
+THE GUARANTEE THE LOCK EXISTS FOR - with the fd closed, a holder that dies releases the lock while its work still
+runs (agents-ttxh). **A STALE LOCK THAT BELIEVES IT IS FREE IS A LOCK NOBODY RECLAIMS.** On timeout the tool says
+LOUDLY that THE WRITE DID NOT LAND, that the lock IS released (true by construction: the group is dead), and that
+the rule is BACK OFF AND RETRY, KILL NOTHING. `124` stays the fleet's "unknown, not a pass" - a killed or timed-out
+command never reads as success.
+**AND THE CONTROL THAT SHIPPED WITH IT HAD THE SAME SHAPE AS THE HAZARD.** A control is built for the case that
+prompted it, and the case that prompts it is always the FAILURE - so the SUCCESS path is the one nobody drives.
+Three instances in a single day: a gate fixture that retained its evidence only on FAILURE and therefore proved
+nothing about the runs that passed; a disk-hygiene timer reporting success while `/tmp` filled; and this very lock,
+released correctly on the timeout path and LEAKED BY A SUCCESSFUL PUSH, because the watchdog's `sleep` inherited the
+fd and killing a subshell does not kill its child - a lock nobody was using, looking exactly like a lock somebody
+was using. The check that catches it belongs on the success path, and it is one line: after a push that returns 0,
+the lock must be free IMMEDIATELY, and no process from the fixture may still hold the fd.
